<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<phpunit xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="vendor/phpunit/phpunit/phpunit.xsd"
         bootstrap="tests/bootstrap.php"
         colors="true"
         cacheDirectory="build/phpunit"
         failOnWarning="true"
         failOnRisky="true"
         beStrictAboutOutputDuringTests="true"
>
    <php>
        <!--
            Generous on purpose, and no longer load-bearing for any test.
            SatisfiabilityEvaluatorTest's wide-policy guard used to need this value:
            the regression it catches — lazy depth-first search reverting to eager
            materialisation of every assignment — is only OBSERVABLE as memory
            exhaustion, since with no limit it hangs rather than failing. Calibrated
            against 128M, that made one test's guard depend on the whole suite's
            limit, with two consequences.

            The pin could not be raised however close the suite came to it, and the
            suite came to within 0.4%: runs began dying at 127.5 MB in PHPUnit's
            result-cache writer AFTER every test had passed, which failed CI on
            allocator noise and named a file no author had touched. And any run that
            raised the limit anyway left the guard reporting green while guarding
            nothing — the mutation campaign raises it to 4G, so it was already inert
            there.

            That guard now runs its evaluation in a subprocess under a limit it sets
            itself, with a control proving the limit bites, so it observes the
            regression at 128M whatever this value is.

            256M is chosen rather than something larger: the suite peaks around 127.5
            MB, so this is roughly double what it needs — loose enough that ordinary
            growth and the result-cache write do not fail a run on noise, tight enough
            that a change which doubles the suite's memory still fails here rather
            than quietly becoming normal. Raise it when the suite genuinely needs the
            room, and say what measured it.
        -->
        <ini name="memory_limit" value="256M"/>
    </php>

    <testsuites>
        <testsuite name="Vouch">
            <directory>tests</directory>
        </testsuite>
    </testsuites>

    <source>
        <include>
            <directory>src</directory>
        </include>
    </source>
</phpunit>
